📜 The Contract
Consistency

If two objects are equal according to equals(), they must return the same hashCode().

If two objects are unequal, they may still return the same hashCode() (hash collisions are allowed, but should be minimized).

Implications

Equal objects → same hash bucket in hash-based collections.

Unequal objects → ideally different hash buckets for efficiency.

⚠️ What Happens If Violated?
Case 1: equals() says two objects are equal, but hashCode() differs

Collections like HashSet or HashMap may place them in different buckets.

Result: Duplicate entries, inability to retrieve stored values, or unexpected behavior.

Case 2: hashCode() is consistent but equals() is broken

Objects may be treated as equal when they shouldn’t be, leading to overwriting or data loss in maps.

🔍 Example
java
class Person {
    String name;

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Person)) return false;
        Person p = (Person) o;
        return name.equals(p.name);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return 42; // BAD: constant hashCode
    }
}
Here, all Person objects have the same hash code.

Even though equals() distinguishes them, they all end up in the same bucket, causing performance degradation (everything becomes a linked list lookup instead of O(1)).

🚨 Key Takeaways
Always override both methods together.

Use fields that define equality in both equals() and hashCode().

Violating the contract leads to bugs in collections and performance issues.
